1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,910
Section 4

2
00:00:01,910 --> 00:00:08,586
In this series of lectures about the history of weather forecasting, I'll start by

3
00:00:08,586 --> 00:00:14,600
examining its early history - that'll be the subject of today's talk.

4
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:15,522
OK,

5
00:00:15,522 --> 00:00:19,268
so we'll start by going back thousands of years.

6
00:00:19,268 --> 00:00:24,184
Most ancient cultures had weather gods,

7
00:00:24,184 --> 00:00:32,570
and weather catastrophes, such as floods, played an important role in many creation myths.

8
00:00:32,570 --> 00:00:39,390
Generally, weather was attributed to the whims of the gods, as the wide range

9
00:00:39,390 --> 00:00:42,800
of weather gods in various cultures shows.

10
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:47,106
For instance, there's the Egyptian sun god Ra,

11
00:00:47,106 --> 00:00:51,950
and Thor, the Norse god of thunder and lightning.

12
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:59,562
Many ancient civilisations developed rites such as dances in order to make the weather

13
00:00:59,562 --> 00:01:02,280
gods look kindly on them.

14
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:06,225
But the weather was of daily importance:

15
00:01:06,225 --> 00:01:14,115
observing the skies and drawing the correct conclusions from these observations was really important,

16
00:01:14,115 --> 00:01:18,060
in fact their survival depended on it.

17
00:01:18,060 --> 00:01:24,730
It isn't known when people first started to observe the skies,

18
00:01:24,730 --> 00:01:36,251
but at around 650 BC, the Babylonians produced the first short-range weather forecasts, based on their observations of clouds

19
00:01:36,251 --> 00:01:38,070
and other phenomena.

20
00:01:38,070 --> 00:01:41,736
The Chinese also recognised weather patterns,

21
00:01:41,736 --> 00:01:50,291
and by 300 BC, astronomers had developed a calendar which divided the year into

22
00:01:50,291 --> 00:01:55,790
24 festivals, each associated with a different weather phenomenon.

23
00:01:55,790 --> 00:02:02,475
The ancient Greeks were the first to develop a more scientific approach to explaining

24
00:02:02,475 --> 00:02:03,430
the weather.

25
00:02:03,430 --> 00:02:11,152
The work of the philosopher and scientist Aristotle, in the fourth century BC, is

26
00:02:11,152 --> 00:02:17,770
especially noteworthy, as his ideas held sway for nearly 2,000 years.

27
00:02:17,770 --> 00:02:25,425
In 340 BC, he wrote a book in which he attempted to account for

28
00:02:25,425 --> 00:02:29,800
the formation of rain, clouds, wind and storms.

29
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:36,945
He also described celestial phenomena such as haloes - that is, bright circles of

30
00:02:36,945 --> 00:02:42,990
light around the sun, the moon and bright stars - and comets.

31
00:02:42,990 --> 00:02:48,000
Many of his observations were surprisingly accurate.

32
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,960
For example, he believed that heat could cause water to evaporate.

33
00:02:54,960 --> 00:03:02,079
But he also jumped to quite a few wrong conclusions, such as that winds

34
00:03:02,079 --> 00:03:05,130
are breathed out by the Earth.

35
00:03:05,130 --> 00:03:10,500
Errors like this were rectified from the Renaissance onwards.

36
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:19,163
For nearly 2,000 years, Aristotle's work was accepted as the chief authority on weather

37
00:03:19,163 --> 00:03:19,740
theory.

38
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:27,339
Alongside this, though, in the Middle Ages weather observations were passed on in the

39
00:03:27,339 --> 00:03:33,310
form of proverbs, such as 'Red sky at night, shepherd's delight;

40
00:03:33,310 --> 00:03:37,110
red sky in the morning, shepherd's warning'.

41
00:03:37,110 --> 00:03:45,312
Many of these are based on very good observations and are accurate as contemporary

42
00:03:45,312 --> 00:03:47,070
meteorologists have discovered.

43
00:03:47,070 --> 00:03:54,670
For centuries, any attempt to forecast the weather could only be based on personal

44
00:03:54,670 --> 00:03:55,213
observations,

45
00:03:55,213 --> 00:04:02,270
but in the fifteenth century scientists began to see the need for instruments.

46
00:04:02,270 --> 00:04:10,215
Until then, the only ones available were weather vanes - to determine the wind direction

47
00:04:10,215 --> 00:04:13,620
and early versions of rain gauges.

48
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:20,628
One of the first, invented in the fifteenth century, was a hygrometer,

49
00:04:20,628 --> 00:04:22,380
which measured humidity.

50
00:04:22,380 --> 00:04:29,830
This was one of many inventions that contributed to the development of weather forecasting.

51
00:04:29,830 --> 00:04:40,030
In 1592, the Italian scientist and inventor Galileo developed the world's first thermometer.

52
00:04:40,030 --> 00:04:44,575
His student Torricelli later invented the barometer,

53
00:04:44,575 --> 00:04:49,120
which allowed people to measure atmospheric pressure.

54
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:57,890
In 1648, the French philosopher Pascal proved that pressure decreases with altitude.

55
00:04:57,890 --> 00:05:04,019
This discovery was verified by English astronomer Halley in 1686;

56
00:05:04,019 --> 00:05:10,760
and Halley was also the first person to map trade winds.

57
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:19,671
This increasing ability to measure factors related to weather helped scientists to understand the atmosphere

58
00:05:19,671 --> 00:05:22,047
and its processes better,

59
00:05:22,047 --> 00:05:26,800
and they started collecting weather observation data systematically.

60
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:34,099
In the eighteenth century, the scientist and politician Benjamin Franklin carried out work on electricity

61
00:05:34,099 --> 00:05:36,045
and lightning in particular,

62
00:05:36,045 --> 00:05:42,857
but he was also very interested in weather and studied it throughout most of

63
00:05:42,857 --> 00:05:43,830
his life.

64
00:05:43,830 --> 00:05:51,420
It was Franklin who discovered that storms generally travel from west to east.

65
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:59,801
In addition to new meteorological instruments, other developments contributed to our understanding of the

66
00:05:59,801 --> 00:06:00,400
atmosphere.

67
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:04,367
People in different locations began to keep records,

68
00:06:04,367 --> 00:06:11,310
and in the mid-nineteenth century, the invention of the telegraph made it possible for

69
00:06:11,310 --> 00:06:13,790
these records to be collated.

70
00:06:13,790 --> 00:06:20,940
This led, by the end of the nineteenth century, to the first weather services.

71
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:27,269
It was not until the early twentieth century that mathematics

72
00:06:27,269 --> 00:06:31,067
and physics became part of meteorology,

73
00:06:31,067 --> 00:06:36,130
and we'll continue from that point next week.
